    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general municipal law, in relation to the municipal sustainable energy financing program",
    "basePrintNo": "S8338",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8338-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Art 5-L Art Head, §§119-ee, 119-ff & 119-gg, add §§119-hh & 119-ii, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8338\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to the municipal\nsustainable energy financing program\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would permit additional Sources of funding for municipal\nsustainable energy financing programs (\"MSEFP\") in New York State\n(\"State\"), provide additional consumer protections for such programs,\nand authorize the City of New York (\"New York City\") to utilize funds\nprovided for its program by a not-for-profit corporation created for\nthat purpose.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend the article heading of General Munici-\npal Law (\"GML\") Article 5-L to read: \"Municipal Sustainable Energy\nFinancing Program.\"\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend GML § 119-ee to include additional\nlegislative findings concerning the State's policy goals of achieving\nenergy independence, preventing future energy shortages, and improving\nair quality and the general health and well-being of the people of the\nState; and delineating the necessity for the New York State Energy\nResearch and Development Authority (\"NYSERDA\") and municipal corpo-\nrations to play a role in achieving such goals.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would amend GML § 119-ff to include additional\ndefinitions for terms used in GML Article 5-L.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would amend GML § 119-gg, as follows:\n\nGML § 119-gg(1) would be amended to authorize any municipality with tax\nenforcement authority that establishes an MSEFP to use, in addition to\nfederal grant assistance and federal credit support, proceeds from bonds\nissued by NYSERDA or other NYSERDA funds including grants and monies\nfrom the Green Jobs-Green New York Fund.\n\nGML § 119-gg(2) would be amended to clarify that real property owners\nare limited to receiving MSEFP-financing for energy efficiency improve-\nments or renewable energy systems through the municipal corporation\nwhere the benefitted real property is located, and that such owners\ncould not receive financing for the same project from more than one\nmunicipal corporation.\n\nGML § 119-gg(3) would be amended to permit New York City to establish\nlists of cost effective energy efficiency improvements for different\nbuilding types that could be financed through the MSEFP.\n\nGML § 119-gg(4) would be amended to permit New York City to establish a\nsystem for verifying and reporting on the installation and performance\nof renewable energy systems and energy efficiency improvements financed\nby the MSEFP.\n\nGML § 119-gg(5) would be amended to provide that a financing made\nthrough the MSEFP must be ,repaid over a term not to exceed the weighted\naverage of the useful life of such systems and improvements as deter-\nmined by NYSERDA pursuant to an agreement with a municipal corporation.\n\nGML § 119-gg(6) would be amended to change the term \"appraised value\" to\n\"fair market value,\" which would be determined by a municipal corpo-\nration or by NYSERDA pursuant to an agreement with a municipal corpo-\nration, for the purpose of determining the maximum amount of financing\navailable under the MSEFP, and to expand the list of eligible costs that\nmay be included in the financing to include issuance costs of bonds, the\ncosts of credit support and all other related transaction and adminis-\ntration costs.\n\nGML § 119-gg(7) would be amended to reflect the use of the term \"financ-\ning\" instead of \"loan.\"\n\nGML § 119-gg(8) would be amended to clarify that a charge imposed pursu-\nant to GML Article 5-L would be and become a lien on the benefitted\nproperty at the same time and in the same manner as municipal taxes;\nthat such lien would have a priority over all other liens and encum-\nbrances on the property, except for liens securing taxes, special ad\nvalorem levies and special assessments; and that in New York City, such\nlien would be subordinate to the lien of taxes, assessments, sewer\nrents, sewer surcharges and water rents, and the interest and charges\nthereon, and on parity with any lien arising from the nonpayment of any\nother charges of such city.\n\nGML § 119-gg(9), which authorizes municipal corporations to require that\nMSEFP financing must be repaid by the property owner through a charge on\nthe real property benefitted by such financing, would be renumbered GML\n§ 119-gg(10) and amended to also provide that all state and local laws\nregarding the enforcement and collection of unpaid real property taxes\nof the municipal corporation would apply to the enforcement and\ncollection of such unpaid charges, and that a municipal corporation may\nenter into agreements with another municipal corporation and NYSERDA\nrelating to the administrative functions of the MSEFP.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(9) would be require the municipal officer responsible\nfor administering the MSEFP, upon the issuance of financing pursuant to\nGML 119-gg, to record with the appropriate recording officer a notice\nstating the existence, duration and amount of such financing.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(11) would require that each MSEFP must disclose the\nconsequences of failing to pay the charge associated with the financing,\nthe effects of transferring ownership of the property, the approximate\nmonthly and yearly charges associated with the MSEFP-financing, the\n\nsavings from the activities funded by the MSEFP-financing, and notifica-\ntion to the affected mortgagees of the charge and the lien associated\nwith such financing.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(12) would prohibit the acceleration of a MSEFP-financ-\ning where a property owner failed to pay a charge on the real property\nbenefitted by such financing, and would require property owners to\ndisclose the MSEFP-financing to any subsequent purchaser, prior to the\nconveyance of his or her property.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(13) would limit MSEFP-financing to: (i) a maximum\nfinancing amount that does not result in the amount of the property\nowner's total debt on the property, including the amount financed,\nexceeding 90% of the fair market value of the property, (ii) property\nthat has no outstanding taxes or involuntary liens, and (iii) installed\nenergy efficiency improvements and renewable energy systems that have a\nsavings-to-investment ratio greater than 1.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(14) would establish eligibility criteria for the MSEFP,\nsetting forth that financing would only be available to property owners\nthat (i) have not failed to pay property taxes during the interest-free\nperiod more than once in the past 3 years, or since the purchase of the\nproperty if less than 3 years, and (ii) have not filed for or declared\n\"bankruptcy during the previous seven years.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(15) would authorize NYSERDA and any municipal corpo-\nration with the power to enforce delinquent tax liens, in order to\nimplement the MSEFP, to enter into a contract to provide for NYSERDA; to\nprovide funding for financings pursuant to this section and for the\nmunicipal corporation to transfer or assign to NYSERDA or its designee\nany funds received by the municipal corporation as a result of charges\nimposed on owners of real property pursuant to GML § 119-gg.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(16) would authorize a municipal corporation to enter\ninto such contracts with third parties, including other municipal corpo-\nrations, to implement the MSEFP upon terms and conditions deemed appro-\npriate by such municipal corporation.\n\nNew GML § 119-gg(17) would provide that nothing in GML Article 5-L would\nprevent a town from electing to proceed under Town Law § 209-i of the\nTown Law.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would amend GML Article 5-L by adding two new\nsections, as follows:\n\nNew GML § 119-hh would:\n\n* authorize New York City to cause the incorporation of one, or more\ncorporations under the not for profit corporation law to provide assist-\nance to it in the implementation of its MSEFP.\n\n* set forth a pledge by the State to the holders of any issue of bonds\nthat the State would not limit, alter or impair the rights and remedies\nof such bondholders or the security for such bonds until such bonds are\nfully paid and discharged.\n\n*establish that bonds issued pursuant to this section are securities in\nwhich various public officers and bodies of the State, public corpo-\nrations, and other entities may properly and legally invest funds.\n\n* exempt an entity described in GML § 119-hh(1) from any fees, taxes,\nspecial ad valorem levies or assessments of any kind, including, but not\nlimited to, franchise taxes, sales taxes or other taxes, upon or with\nrespect to any property owned by it.  GML § 19-hh(4) would also exempt\nfrom taxation the bonds of such entity together with the income there-\nfrom.\n\nNew GML § 119-ii would provide a severability provision for GML Article\n5-L.\n\nSection 6 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nChapter 497 of the Laws of 2009 authorizes the legislative body of any\nmunicipal corporation to establish, by local law, a sustainable energy\nloan program using federal grant assistance or federal credit support\navailable for this purpose.\n\nChapter 409 of the Laws of 2009 authorizes town refuse and garbage\ndistricts to provide financing to residential property owners for energy\naudits and energy efficiency improvements.\n\nChapter 344 of the Laws of 2009 authorizes the City of Binghamton to\nestablish a sustainable energy loan program using federal grants avail-\nable for this purpose.\n\nChapter 336 of the Laws of 2009 authorizes the Town of Bedford to estab-\nlish a sustainable energy loan program using federal grants available\nfor this purpose.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nIn his 2009 State of the State address, Governor Paterson outlined his\nplan to meet 45% of the State's electricity needs through clean renewa-\nble energy and improved energy efficiency by 2015 (\"45 by 15\").  During\nthe 2009 special session, the Legislature enacted one component of the\n45 by 15 plan by authorizing municipalities to promote and finance the\ninstallation of renewable energy systems and energy efficiency using\n\nfederal funds. This bill would expand the financial resources available\nto the State's MSEFP in order to better combat climate change.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediately.\n\n",
